from pathlib import Path
import json
R=Path('/data/agents/nissy-throughput-20261108');A=json.loads((R/'analysis.json').read_text());raw=str(R)+'/raw/'
if A['status']!='COMPLETE':raise SystemExit('Incomplete measurements')
if json.loads((R/'cross-replay.json').read_text())['egg_model']['verified']!=15000:raise SystemExit('Cross replay missing')
text=(R/'src/NISSY_METHODS.md').read_text();text=text.replace('2026-11-08. **Measurements are in progress.** Final repeated results will replace this status after every table completes. No partial row is a final speedup claim.','2026-11-08. Complete: five table sizes, three final 500-state batches per solver and size, on the same 48-core VM. These results describe this dataset, machine, source and configuration grid.')
results='## Final throughput\n\nMedians of three complete batch walls, after tuning. Each final process solves all 500 states. Higher solves/s is better.\n\n| h | Egg batch wall | Nissy batch wall | Egg solves/s | Nissy solves/s | Nissy time / egg time |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for h,p in A['finals'].items():
 e,n=p['egg'],p['nissy'];results+=f"| {h} | {e['median_wall_seconds']:.6f} s | {n['median_wall_seconds']:.6f} s | {e['solves_per_second']:.2f} | {n['solves_per_second']:.2f} | {p['speedup_egg_over_nissy']:.2f}× |\n"
results+='\nEvery selected egg configuration uses 48 workers, 24 lanes per worker, and concurrency zero (the complete input batch). Nissy uses 6 cube workers × 8 threads/cube for h6–h8, and 12 × 4 for h9–h10. Its selected build is retained PGO for h6/h8/h9/h10 and upstream default compiler flags for h7. PGO training overlap and the upstream rank issue are disclosed below.\n\n## Every final raw output\n\nThe links contain unedited per-state results and the total line. The value shown is that run\'s complete batch wall. Three repeats are 1,500 solves of the same 500 distinct states, not 1,500 distinct states.\n\n| h | Solver | Configuration | Repeat 1, raw | Repeat 2, raw | Repeat 3, raw |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for h,p in A['finals'].items():
 for solver in ['egg','nissy']:
  x=p[solver];links=[f'[{wall:.9f} s]({raw}{rid}.jsonl)' for rid,wall in zip(x['runs'],x['wall_seconds'])];results+=f"| {h} | {solver} | `{x['config']}` | "+' | '.join(links)+' |\n'
results+=f'\nAll {A["accepted_measurements"]} accepted measurements, including the exploratory probe and configuration screens, appear in [the run index]({R}/run-index.csv). The [analysis JSON]({R}/analysis.json), [manifest]({R}/manifest.json) and [cross-replay result]({R}/cross-replay.json) are machine-readable.\n\n'
text=text.replace('## Workload and metric',results+'## Workload and metric')
text=text.replace('Additional cross-implementation replay is recorded separately.','All 15,000 final solutions additionally replayed in both independent cube models: [cross-replay evidence]('+str(R)+'/cross-replay.json).')
text=text.replace('Reported final values will be medians', 'Reported final values are medians')
text=text.replace('may already contain alignment padding', 'contain alignment padding')
text=text.replace('recorded separately in the manifest', 'recorded separately in the per-table metadata and `restored-tables.json`')
text=text.replace('## Raw evidence','## Interpreting this comparison\n\nThe historical reports contain different source builds, sample sizes and latency/stream formulas. Their ratios must not be substituted for this finite-batch result. In particular, this is a fresh nissy comparison, not a new measurement of closed-source cubeopt or the reported MacBook release. These numbers do not establish a cross-machine or universal solver ranking.\n\nThe build screen and allocation screen are separate: builds were screened with 48 independent single-thread workers, then the selected build\'s allocations were screened on all 500 states. Every compiler build was not exhaustively crossed with every allocation. Lane counts 12 and 20 were not in this experiment\'s explicit lane grid. “Best observed” means exactly the recorded search, not every possible parameter combination.\n\n## Raw evidence')
parallel=(R/'src/PARALLELISM_DRAFT.md').read_text().replace('Counter interpretation and the initial h6 measurements are complete; the h6–h10 throughput matrix and strong-scaling measurements are still running. Final results will be appended with their raw records.','Counter interpretation, the h6–h10 finite-batch matrix, and the single-cube scaling measurements are complete. Both final solution sets pass cross-implementation replay.')
parallel=parallel.replace('The new experiment explicitly tests 64, 96 and 128 lanes as well as smaller values.','The new experiment explicitly tested 64, 96 and 128 lanes as well as smaller values.')
parallel=parallel.replace('The matrix records the fastest observed complete-batch configuration, and separate one-cube-at-a-time scaling runs at 1–48 CPU threads. Speedup and efficiency will be reported from those measured times, alongside changed node counts.','The matrix records the fastest observed complete-batch configuration, and separate one-cube-at-a-time scaling runs at 1–48 CPU threads. Measured speedup, efficiency and changed node counts are below.')
section='## Production CLI lane screen\n\nThese are actual complete-batch walls for all 500 states, one process per tuning configuration, 48 CPU workers. The shared-work CLI is used throughout this table. It fixes worker-state and stack-frame placement; `run_pinned` does not mean CPU-affinity pinning. The ordinary research-probe path above is excluded from production configuration selection.\n\n| Lanes/worker | h6 seconds | h7 seconds | h8 seconds | h9 seconds | h10 seconds |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for lane in [8,16,24,32,40,48,64,96,128]:
 values=[]
 for h in range(6,11):
  row=json.loads((R/'meta'/f'h{h}-egg-48_{lane}_0-all500-tune-cli-lanes.json').read_text());values.append(f"{row['total']['wall']:.4f}")
 section+=f'| {lane} | '+' | '.join(values)+' |\n'
section+='\nTwenty-four lanes won each screen. Neither a 48-lane limit nor larger defaults are justified by these measurements: 64–128 lanes are supported but slower on this batch. The existing built-in tune grid still ends at 48; explicit larger values remain usable. Other hardware and workload sizes need their own tuning.\n\nThe probe/CLI timing gap is also a useful warning: an algorithmically similar research caller is not interchangeable with the shipped program. Fixed placement and different generated code are possible contributors; this VM has no PMU evidence that isolates their contributions. We do not attribute the entire difference to one mechanism.\n\n## Measured single-cube scaling\n\nThis study solves one input at a time (`-c 1`) with the production CLI. It uses three declared states (indices 0, 8 and 10, optimal lengths 18, 16 and 19), repeated ten times in the same order: 30 solves, three distinct states. The 24-lane count is fixed across worker counts; it was selected by the throughput screen, not retuned for every scaling row. Each row is one process, so small differences are exploratory rather than repeated confidence intervals.\n\n| CPU workers | h6 ms/cube | h6 speedup | h6 efficiency | h10 ms/cube | h10 speedup | h10 efficiency |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for x,y in zip(A['scaling']['6'],A['scaling']['10']):
 section+=f"| {x['threads']} | {x['ms_per_cube']:.3f} | {x['speedup']:.2f}× | {x['efficiency']:.1%} | {y['ms_per_cube']:.3f} | {y['speedup']:.2f}× | {y['efficiency']:.1%} |\n"
section+='\nAt 48 workers, the measured speedup was **33.28× for h6** and **30.37× for h10**: 69.3% and 63.3% efficiency against the one-worker configuration. The searches counted 19.2% and 6.7% more nodes respectively than at one worker. This extra work, imbalance and coordination mean the figures cannot identify a universal parallel fraction by an Amdahl fit.\n\nThis establishes useful parallelism within one cube through 48 CPU workers on these states. Twenty-four lanes × 48 workers configures 1,152 interleaved contexts; it is not a claim that all are continuously occupied or executed simultaneously. Both implementations can generate tens of thousands of prefix jobs, and split work further. Their usefulness depends on pruning, target depth, the remaining tail and scheduling cost. This CPU study neither measures a GPU nor determines its maximum useful width.\n\nThe nissy allocation screen makes the batch issue concrete too: h6\'s pilot took 60.624 s at 48 × 1 versus 21.796 s at 6 × 8. The latter finished the all-500 confirmation in 102.621 s and won the complete allocation screen. Allocating all cores to independent inputs is not always the best way to finish a finite batch.\n\nRaw single-cube outputs are indexed by `*-single30-parallelism` in [the run index]('+str(R)+'/run-index.csv); [analysis JSON]('+str(R)+'/analysis.json) retains every speedup and node ratio. Final h6–h10 throughput and all 30 raw final outputs are in [the nissy comparison](NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md).\n\n'
parallel=parallel.replace('## Benchmark scope',section+'## Benchmark scope')
Path('/home/super/NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md').write_text(text)
Path('/home/super/PARALLELISM.md').write_text(parallel.replace('(NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md)','(/home/super/NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md)'))
for root in [Path('/home/super/egg-solver'),Path('/data/agents/cubeopt-current/egg')]:
 (root/'docs/comparisons/NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md').write_text(text.replace(str(R)+'/', '../data/nissy-throughput-20261108/'))
 (root/'docs/performance/PARALLELISM.md').write_text(parallel.replace(str(R)+'/', '../data/nissy-throughput-20261108/').replace('(NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md)','(../comparisons/NISSY_THROUGHPUT.md)'))
print('Reports written')
